WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:03.937 align:start size:97% position:3%
- The margin presented a
target of opportunity for us.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:06.573 align:start size:88% position:13%
Look, like I said,
this is my fourth term,

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:08.975
and I'm tired of the
way this place works.

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:10.477
I think it's deeply corrupt.

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:12.679
I think the lobbyists run
the show most of the times.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.347 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think people are
more interested

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.649
in servicing their
PAC fundraiser than
they are the needs

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:17.951
of their constituents.

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:19.853
And it's a grift game.

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:22.522
And what facilitates
that grift game is

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:25.392
an entire sophisticated
structure that ensures

00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:28.828 align:start position:0%
that no one's really
responsible for the legislating.

00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:31.564
(dramatic music)

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:36.336
- All right.

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:37.203
We're still in DC.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:38.171
I'm still Dave Rubin.

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:39.439
It's still the Rubin Report.

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:41.441
And joining me today
is the congressman

00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:43.576
from Florida's first district

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.779
and a first time
Rubin Report guest,

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:46.613
which is really bizarre to me,

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:47.447
Matt Gaetz.

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:48.281
Matt, how are you?

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:49.115
- Good to see you.

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:50.650
Thanks for having me.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.085
- This is weird to me that
we've never sat down before.

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:53.653 align:start position:0%
I feel like you've been
fighting with everybody, and...

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:56.623 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I feel like I know you
because I watch the show, so.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:57.924
- How am I doing?

00:00:57.924 --> 00:00:59.592
- Great. I mean, you're
getting the message out.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:00.827
- Killing it in the hair game.

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:02.095
That's really what I
wanna focus on mostly.

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:04.631
- You know, when Dave
Brat lost in Virginia,

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:07.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
it was sad to see a
colleague I respected go,

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:09.702 align:start size:94% position:6%
but that was when I got
the best hair in Congress,

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.305 align:start size:84% position:16%
and so there's
some up and comers.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:16.109
Kiley out of California, he's
got great hair, great height.

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:17.577
I'm worried about him.

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:19.079 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Is that mostly what's
going on behind the scenes?

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:20.513
Like everyone's like
who has the best hair?

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.115
Who has the worst hair plugs?

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:23.383 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's pretty much
what's happening, right?

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:24.951
- I wish there was
a border bill up.

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:27.754
How about that for a lead?

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:31.691
I wish that we were talking
about internal enforcement

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:33.193
and asylum reform.

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:38.031
One thing that frustrates me
a little is that we've passed

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:41.434 align:start position:0%
two bills to deal with the
borders of Indian tribes and not

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.736 align:start size:94% position:6%
yet a bill to deal with
the border of our country.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:48.708 align:start position:0%
So I wouldn't give our congress
an A-plus yet until we get

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:51.111
the work of the people done.

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:52.078
- I like it. You're going
right into the issues.

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:53.546
So let's do it right there.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:55.715 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, actually gimme one
minute on just Matt Gaetz 101

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:57.784 align:start size:97% position:3%
for the people that just don't
know your history at all.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:01:59.018
- Oh, sure.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:00.286
- And then we'll get
into all the specifics.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:01.988 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm a country lawyer
from North Florida,

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:04.991 align:start position:0%
served in our state legislature
proudly for six years.

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:07.127 align:start position:0%
I chaired what was the
equivalent of our Ways and Means

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:10.864 align:start size:97% position:3%
Committee, fought for low
taxes, good regulatory reform.

00:02:10.864 --> 00:02:14.267 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was probably a more
libertarian-leaning
member of the

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.603
State House than I
am of the Congress,

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:21.040
but when President Trump was
running, the juices started to

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.909
stir to do a little
more for my country.

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:23.743
I ran.

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.245
I got here.

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:28.848
I'm in my fourth term now and
eager to continue the work.

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.818 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that we've got
one-half of one-third of the

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:35.622 align:start position:0%
government right now, and every
day is really a test in how

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:39.225 align:start size:84% position:16%
to exercise leverage
effectively, and you
can overplay that

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:42.762
and underplay that, but we're
working to curate that balance

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:45.265 align:start size:88% position:13%
in a way that makes
life better for people.

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:46.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So let's talk about that
leverage for a minute,

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
because just about two hours
ago I had Congressman Dan

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:52.705 align:start size:97% position:3%
Crenshaw in here, and you guys
were obviously on opposite

00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:56.743
sides of everything that went
down with McCarthy and the

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:59.245 align:start size:97% position:3%
leadership, but that was my
whole purpose of coming to DC.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.748 align:start size:97% position:3%
I wanted to sit down with guys
that were on every side of

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:03.016
every issue.

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:06.519
So you basically led the
fight against McCarthy.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:09.222
Were you planning on doing that?

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:11.057 align:start size:97% position:3%
Did it just kind of
materialize out of nowhere?

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:13.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then did it become
like a monster unto itself

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:15.228
once it started?

00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:17.497
- Well, I think there were
a lot of people who took

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:20.400
leadership roles on
different issue sets.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.270 align:start size:97% position:3%
Not every person required
the same concessions to allow

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.239
McCarthy to ascend
to the speakership.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.041 align:start size:81% position:19%
Some people are
more fiscal hawks,

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:29.776 align:start size:81% position:19%
some people are
more border hawks,

00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:31.544
some people are oversight hawks.

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:34.080
And so within those different
groups, there were a lot of

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.916
voices that emerged and so I
wouldn't characterize myself

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:39.052 align:start size:97% position:3%
the leader, but we
realized the opportunity...

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:40.553 align:start position:0%
- Well, I guess maybe the media
at least kind of framed you

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:42.789
as you were kind of the one...

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:43.990
- The media does like
talking about me a lot.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.225 align:start size:81% position:19%
- They do like
talking about you.

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:46.859
Well, you hang out with
the orange guy a lot.

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:48.428 align:start size:81% position:19%
There's a lot,
you know, come on.

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:51.698
- Well, you know, he's a
fun guy to hang out with.

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:56.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
So the margin presented a
target of opportunity for us.

00:03:57.604 --> 00:03:59.138
Look, like I said,

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:01.574 align:start size:97% position:3%
this is my fourth term and I'm
tired of the way this place

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:02.508
works.

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:04.010
I think it's deeply corrupt.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.212
I think the lobbyists run
the show most of the times.

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:08.481
I think people are
more interested in
servicing their PAC

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:11.284
fundraiser than they are the
needs of their constituents.

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:13.419
And it's a grift game.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:16.923
And what facilitates that
grift game is an entire

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:19.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
sophisticated structure
that ensures that no one's

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.961
really responsible for
the legislating, right?

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:26.099
If we had to take individual
votes on individual departments

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:30.970
and authorizations and bills
and spending, then we would

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:35.074
probably be held to account
to a greater degree by the

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:36.376
people in this country.

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:39.579
But instead what you get
is every year or so some

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:43.216
multi-thousand page bill that
you get a day, day and a half

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:46.753 align:start size:97% position:3%
to read, and the whole deal
is thumbs up or thumbs down on

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:48.288
the whole thing.

00:04:48.288 --> 00:04:51.691
And then you're left with the
decision of voting for a bunch

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:54.527 align:start size:97% position:3%
of crap you don't agree with
or leaving out the widows

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:56.696 align:start position:0%
and the orphans and the veterans
and the military and things

00:04:56.696 --> 00:04:59.098
that every American
would care about.

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:03.836
And we had to force upon the
system a better way to deal

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:06.906
with legislating
as an enterprise.

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:10.910 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that had features in
policy, features in personnel,

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:12.345
and features in procedure,

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:13.813
and that's what we
were working through.

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:16.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So I heard you on Tim Pool's
podcast talking about just

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:18.951 align:start size:97% position:3%
that, these massive bills that
you get and that nobody can

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.320
read it as you just laid out,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.554
and it's a whole bunch of stuff,

00:05:21.554 --> 00:05:22.789
but if you don't vote for it,

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:24.290
someone's gonna
die and all that.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.392
So I wanna talk about
the specifics of that,

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:29.295
but did you feel like you
actually at the end got the

00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:31.364 align:start position:0%
concessions, and how do you
know that you're actually gonna

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:32.198
get them?

00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:33.733
You know what I mean?

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:35.168
Like do you have a piece of
paper that's sitting in your

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:37.704 align:start size:94% position:6%
office, you and McCarthy,
to go on this stuff.

00:05:37.704 --> 00:05:40.206 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, the ultimate
accountability tool
isn't a piece of

00:05:40.206 --> 00:05:41.674
paper.

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:43.176 align:start size:97% position:3%
The accountability tool is
that any one member can go and

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.610
force a vote on the
speaker at any time

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.612
and, you know, throw it...

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:49.015 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So that was the main
concession, right?

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:51.584 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That was the enforcement
tool for the other substantive

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:53.086
concessions, right?

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:55.254
But I mean, I'll gladly go
into the substance of these

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:58.124
things and, sure, we
add documents that
we exchange back and

00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:00.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
forth with offers and
suggestions and revisions.

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:04.030
In any negotiation, you never
get exactly what you want.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:08.901
But I recall going into
the speaker's office with

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:12.872 align:start size:97% position:3%
Scott Perry and Lauren Boebert
before the balloting began,

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:15.808 align:start position:0%
and we laid out what we
thought was a pretty reasonable

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:17.410
proposal to the speaker.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:21.614
And he laughed at us and said
we just wanted the opportunity

00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:24.317 align:start size:97% position:3%
to have that power, but
that resided in the speaker,

00:06:24.317 --> 00:06:25.885
not in our group.

00:06:25.885 --> 00:06:30.790
And then ultimately we got to
something that was far better

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:33.960
than had been rejected and
ultimately far better for the

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.628
House of Representatives.

00:06:35.628 --> 00:06:37.597
And I don't say that as
a criticism of McCarthy.

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:39.632 align:start size:97% position:3%
I actually think it's to
his great credit that he was

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:43.503 align:start position:0%
willing to see past a reflexive
opposition to things like

00:06:43.503 --> 00:06:46.272
the one member motion
to vacate the chair.

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:48.274
He had originally said
that was a red line.

00:06:48.274 --> 00:06:49.208
He would never cross it.

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:50.476
He would never go there.

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:52.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
And had he been
stubborn about that,

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:55.281 align:start position:0%
he probably wouldn't be speaker,
and it would've been worse

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:57.683
for the country and for the
House and for the party.

00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:01.621 align:start size:97% position:3%
So his ability to, I think,
move past his red lines

00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:04.090
started that process
of building trust.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.526 align:start position:0%
- What do you make how it
creates strange bedfellows outta

00:07:06.526 --> 00:07:09.562 align:start size:94% position:6%
people. So for example,
Trump is backing McCarthy,

00:07:09.562 --> 00:07:13.132 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you're a Trump guy,
you're going against McCarthy.

00:07:13.132 --> 00:07:14.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
The base doesn't
seem to like McCarthy

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:16.469
but the base loves Trump.

00:07:16.469 --> 00:07:20.306
There were so many just odd
pieces to the whole thing.

00:07:20.306 --> 00:07:23.376 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I guess. I mean, we're
complicated people.

00:07:23.376 --> 00:07:25.711 align:start size:84% position:16%
No two people see
everything the same.

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:28.614
I know people view me
as a Trump acolyte,

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:30.450
and certainly I spend time
with the former president.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:31.951
I like him, I support him,

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:35.288
but even President Trump and
I see some issues differently.

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:39.358
And when you have a high
intensity negotiation,

00:07:39.358 --> 00:07:41.427
different people can go to
different corners and make

00:07:41.427 --> 00:07:43.196 align:start size:84% position:16%
different demands,
and I think ultimately

00:07:43.196 --> 00:07:44.697
we got to a good result,

00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:49.602 align:start position:0%
but I don't believe that anyone
deserves praise yet because

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.372 align:start size:84% position:16%
all we have developed
is a toolkit,

00:07:53.372 --> 00:07:56.676 align:start position:0%
and it's a toolkit that's really
unlike any I've ever had as

00:07:56.676 --> 00:07:58.177
a member of Congress.

00:07:58.177 --> 00:08:00.413
But if all we do is admire
the tools and talk about how

00:08:00.413 --> 00:08:03.216
great it was that we got the
tools and what a negotiation

00:08:03.216 --> 00:08:06.385 align:start size:97% position:3%
delivered the tools to the
toolkit and we don't use them,

00:08:06.385 --> 00:08:08.888
then we are part of the
fraudulent system that

00:08:08.888 --> 00:08:10.089
screws people over.

00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:12.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So how do you
wanna use the tools?

00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:14.227
Today, you're in DC.

00:08:14.227 --> 00:08:15.228
If you go over to the capitol,

00:08:15.228 --> 00:08:16.629
start chatting with these guys,

00:08:16.629 --> 00:08:18.798 align:start size:97% position:3%
how do you want to actually
exercise this stuff?

00:08:18.798 --> 00:08:20.933 align:start position:0%
- Well, one that's got me a
little worked up as we're having

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:22.935
this discussion is the border.

00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:26.606 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's a commitment that
Speaker McCarthy made that the

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:29.709 align:start position:0%
border plan that had been
previously endorsed by the Texas

00:08:29.709 --> 00:08:33.112
delegation would be
coming to the floor.

00:08:33.112 --> 00:08:38.017 align:start position:0%
And right now we don't even
have a border vote scheduled in

00:08:39.252 --> 00:08:40.753
the House Judiciary Committee.

00:08:40.753 --> 00:08:44.590
We were supposed to right now
be preparing for an upcoming

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.660 align:start size:84% position:16%
hearing, and the
immigration bills were

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:48.895
taken off the agenda.

00:08:48.895 --> 00:08:50.530 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I'm a little
frustrated about that.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:53.432
I want to give grace in
deference where it's due,

00:08:53.432 --> 00:08:55.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
if there are some details
still to work out.

00:08:55.401 --> 00:08:59.305
But one substantive concession
is to get a bill to the floor

00:08:59.305 --> 00:09:02.275 align:start position:0%
that says that we detain people
if we have the space or we

00:09:02.275 --> 00:09:03.376
turn them away.

00:09:03.376 --> 00:09:04.477
It's H.R. 29.

00:09:05.912 --> 00:09:09.615
And we need to get to the
business of doing that.

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:11.617
The other is the budget.

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:15.488 align:start size:88% position:13%
We have to start
moving individual
appropriations bills to

00:09:15.488 --> 00:09:18.190 align:start size:84% position:16%
the floor so we can
evaluate every agency,

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.293
every layer of every agency.

00:09:20.293 --> 00:09:22.328 align:start size:88% position:13%
That has to start
getting scheduled in the

00:09:22.328 --> 00:09:23.563
Appropriations Committee.

00:09:23.563 --> 00:09:25.665
We were promised a
vote on term limits.

00:09:25.665 --> 00:09:27.934 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think that a lot of
these things, by the way,

00:09:27.934 --> 00:09:29.535 align:start size:84% position:16%
are gonna fail that
I'm asking for.

00:09:29.535 --> 00:09:30.469
- Right.

00:09:30.469 --> 00:09:31.203
- Term limits vote will fail.

00:09:31.203 --> 00:09:32.672
- Right.

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:35.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
- The real changes to secure
the border might fail if

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:38.144 align:start size:88% position:13%
pro-amnesty Republicans
don't hang with us.

00:09:38.144 --> 00:09:39.912
The bottom line is
I'm okay with that.

00:09:39.912 --> 00:09:42.648
You see the leadership in
both parties have this view

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:45.017 align:start size:84% position:16%
that it's this great
embarrassment and
this great tumult

00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:46.852
if you put something on the
floor that doesn't have the

00:09:46.852 --> 00:09:48.054
votes.

00:09:48.054 --> 00:09:49.522 align:start size:84% position:16%
I actually think that
can be revealing.

00:09:49.522 --> 00:09:52.825
And sometimes you have to go
through an iterative process to

00:09:52.825 --> 00:09:54.827
get to a good work product.

00:09:54.827 --> 00:09:59.498
And whether it's Obamacare or
any number of other bills that

00:09:59.498 --> 00:10:01.434
don't get that scrutiny,

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:03.469 align:start position:0%
you end up with all these
terrible unintended consequences

00:10:03.469 --> 00:10:04.937
if you don't do it.

00:10:04.937 --> 00:10:06.739
- So when you talk about
these freaking giant, well,

00:10:06.739 --> 00:10:08.374 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're not like this. They're
actually like almost to the

00:10:08.374 --> 00:10:10.142
ceiling high, some
of these things.

00:10:10.142 --> 00:10:11.677
So they hand this to you,

00:10:11.677 --> 00:10:15.147 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you basically don't know
who's written it, right?

00:10:15.147 --> 00:10:16.816
Or it's lobbyists that
have half-written it.

00:10:16.816 --> 00:10:18.084
- I know who's written it. Yeah.

00:10:18.084 --> 00:10:18.918
- It's the interest groups.

00:10:18.918 --> 00:10:20.386
- Yeah.

00:10:20.386 --> 00:10:24.523
- I mean, what this town runs
on are the interest groups

00:10:24.523 --> 00:10:26.759
that plow money into
the political system.

00:10:26.759 --> 00:10:28.995 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it's more
sophisticated than that,

00:10:28.995 --> 00:10:30.963
but that is really the rock
upon which they built their

00:10:30.963 --> 00:10:32.198
church.

00:10:32.198 --> 00:10:34.367
But it's also like in
a lot of these offices,

00:10:34.367 --> 00:10:37.203
you get underpaid staff
working for the taxpayer.

00:10:37.203 --> 00:10:39.772
And a lot of them their dream
is to go work as lobbyists

00:10:39.772 --> 00:10:41.207
or for these interest groups.

00:10:41.207 --> 00:10:44.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
So they end up in some
places becoming the valets of

00:10:44.644 --> 00:10:46.045
the special interests.

00:10:46.045 --> 00:10:47.046
Now, fortunately, my staff is...

00:10:47.046 --> 00:10:48.714
we're a pirate ship,

00:10:48.714 --> 00:10:50.449
so everybody comes on board
and understands that we got a

00:10:50.449 --> 00:10:51.984
different kind of mission.

00:10:51.984 --> 00:10:55.421
But in a lot of places, that
is actually part of the swamp

00:10:55.421 --> 00:10:57.223
that concerns me.

00:10:57.223 --> 00:10:59.659
But they're able to hide
in that corrupt system.

00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:02.328
If you have individual
scrutiny of the bills,

00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:04.697 align:start size:84% position:16%
you get to flush
out all the varmint,

00:11:04.697 --> 00:11:06.365
and then you know what happens.

00:11:06.365 --> 00:11:10.836 align:start position:0%
And getting personnel appointed
is another critical thing

00:11:10.836 --> 00:11:12.271
for conservatives.

00:11:12.271 --> 00:11:16.308 align:start position:0%
We have people like Thomas
Massie, Chip Roy, Ralph Norman,

00:11:16.308 --> 00:11:20.012
who we trust do not fall
victim to that system.

00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:22.048
They have long histories
of standing up for their

00:11:22.048 --> 00:11:24.083
constituents, and so we put
the three of them on the

00:11:24.083 --> 00:11:24.917
Rules Committee.

00:11:24.917 --> 00:11:26.318
Now guess what?

00:11:26.318 --> 00:11:27.887
Before stuff gets to the floor,

00:11:27.887 --> 00:11:29.822
we're working out our
issues in advance,

00:11:29.822 --> 00:11:33.759 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're getting some of the pork
and special favors for the

00:11:33.759 --> 00:11:35.695
lobby corps weeded out.

00:11:35.695 --> 00:11:38.831 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then on the
Appropriations Committee,

00:11:38.831 --> 00:11:41.934
we also demanded spots for
additional conservatives.

00:11:41.934 --> 00:11:46.005
We demanded that one of our
existing members have reins

00:11:46.005 --> 00:11:48.374 align:start size:97% position:3%
over a subcommittee to be
able to constrain some of the

00:11:48.374 --> 00:11:50.042
social spending.

00:11:50.042 --> 00:11:51.911 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, I mean, these
are real concessions
that Speaker McCarthy

00:11:51.911 --> 00:11:53.446
has delivered on.

00:11:53.446 --> 00:11:56.115
And then we've just got a lot
of other stuff gestating in

00:11:56.115 --> 00:11:59.151 align:start size:88% position:13%
the pipeline and, as you
can tell, I'm anxious

00:11:59.151 --> 00:12:00.920
to get that done.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:03.689
- What do you think
the Republicans can
do to message this

00:12:03.689 --> 00:12:05.624
stuff better because it's...

00:12:05.624 --> 00:12:08.461 align:start position:0%
I've been telling all my guests,
and I'm gonna sit down with

00:12:08.461 --> 00:12:09.962
with Chip and Thomas tomorrow.

00:12:09.962 --> 00:12:11.697
Ron Johnson was here today.

00:12:11.697 --> 00:12:13.566 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm sitting down with
as many people as I can.

00:12:13.566 --> 00:12:16.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
And one of the things that
seems odd to me is that

00:12:16.368 --> 00:12:18.571
Peter Thiel always says
we have the evil party

00:12:18.571 --> 00:12:20.072
and the stupid party,

00:12:20.072 --> 00:12:22.174 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it's like the Democrats
are the evil party, obviously.

00:12:22.174 --> 00:12:24.210
But then there's this other
party that, as frustrated as

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:27.780
people may be with them, they
just don't seem to want to

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:29.148
cross that divide.

00:12:29.148 --> 00:12:31.150 align:start size:97% position:3%
What do you think
Republicans can do on that?

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:33.519
- You know, we face
exquisite headwinds.

00:12:33.519 --> 00:12:36.188 align:start size:94% position:6%
The censorship industrial
complex is real,

00:12:36.188 --> 00:12:38.591
and actually we're using
the power we have in the

00:12:38.591 --> 00:12:40.493
Weaponization subcommittee,

00:12:40.493 --> 00:12:43.462
also a concession that was a
part of the speaker's race,

00:12:43.462 --> 00:12:46.599 align:start size:97% position:3%
to expose that censorship
industrial complex to haul the

00:12:46.599 --> 00:12:49.602
people forward that really
are the puppet masters that

00:12:49.602 --> 00:12:52.805
engage in the shadow banning
and the programming of the

00:12:52.805 --> 00:12:55.808 align:start size:84% position:16%
mainstream media and
the government threats
to big tech that

00:12:55.808 --> 00:12:58.377
shape what people see
and what they believe.

00:12:58.377 --> 00:13:01.480
So it's hard to talk about
the efficacy of one's message

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:04.316
when you're not talking about
the systems that proliferate

00:13:04.316 --> 00:13:05.785
that message.

00:13:05.785 --> 00:13:09.455 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I think that is a threshold
issue you have to address.

00:13:09.455 --> 00:13:12.658 align:start position:0%
Look, you always want a
movement with the best messages

00:13:12.658 --> 00:13:14.193
and the best messengers.

00:13:14.193 --> 00:13:18.664
And fortunately for us, the
left is somewhere between a

00:13:19.832 --> 00:13:23.969
nursing home escape and
a carnival freak show.

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:25.237
- Yeah.

00:13:25.237 --> 00:13:26.505
- And so we've got a
lot of running room,

00:13:26.505 --> 00:13:28.240
and I actually think we
have great messengers.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:30.743
I mean, you mentioned
Dan Crenshaw earlier.

00:13:30.743 --> 00:13:33.012 align:start size:97% position:3%
There are a lot of issues that
he and I see differently in

00:13:33.012 --> 00:13:35.147
the House, but that's a guy
who knows how to carry a

00:13:35.147 --> 00:13:36.081
message.

00:13:36.081 --> 00:13:36.882
He does it so effectively.

00:13:36.882 --> 00:13:38.150
I admire that about him.

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:39.385
- He actually said the
same thing about you.

00:13:39.385 --> 00:13:40.286
So there you go.

00:13:40.286 --> 00:13:41.821
- Yeah.

00:13:41.821 --> 00:13:45.758
And I think that right now the
interesting exercise we're in

00:13:45.758 --> 00:13:49.028
with a four-seat majority is
we have to think about things

00:13:49.028 --> 00:13:53.299
that we can unify behind
that go from Matt Gaetz to

00:13:55.167 --> 00:13:58.370 align:start position:0%
a Mike Lawler or a Brian
Fitzpatrick or a David Valadeo,

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:00.706
someone more in the
political center.

00:14:00.706 --> 00:14:03.042
And acknowledging that and
working through that actually

00:14:03.042 --> 00:14:04.910
makes us stronger as
a team going forward.

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:07.613
And I don't say that as
some cheeky bullshit.

00:14:07.613 --> 00:14:10.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
You look at the debt limit
that we have right now.

00:14:10.349 --> 00:14:12.551
What are we gonna demand as
a part of the negotiation on

00:14:12.551 --> 00:14:14.053
the debt limit?

00:14:14.053 --> 00:14:17.256
I've proposed demanding work
requirements on a lot of these

00:14:17.256 --> 00:14:18.958
social safety net programs,

00:14:18.958 --> 00:14:20.926 align:start size:94% position:6%
not because that's the
only thing I'd like to do.

00:14:20.926 --> 00:14:23.462 align:start position:0%
I'd like to zero out the
Federal Department of Education.

00:14:23.462 --> 00:14:25.497
I'd like to zero out the ATF.

00:14:25.497 --> 00:14:27.566 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'd like to zero out
the Department of Labor.

00:14:27.566 --> 00:14:29.034
I'd like to do all those things,

00:14:29.034 --> 00:14:33.606 align:start position:0%
but I recognize that those
demands are likely to fizzle in

00:14:33.606 --> 00:14:36.775
the broader construct we find
ourselves in with Biden in the

00:14:36.775 --> 00:14:39.011
White House and with the
Senate in Democrat hands.

00:14:39.011 --> 00:14:42.314
And so what are the ways that
we can juxtapose Joe Biden,

00:14:42.314 --> 00:14:46.085 align:start position:0%
not to me, but to Bill Clinton,
who was willing to sit down

00:14:46.085 --> 00:14:47.720
and work out work requirements.

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:52.324 align:start size:88% position:13%
So that's how a
four-seat majority
informs on the real-time

00:14:52.324 --> 00:14:54.693
decisions we're making this
week about a consequential

00:14:54.693 --> 00:14:56.295
issue like the debt limit.

00:14:56.295 --> 00:14:58.597 align:start position:0%
- So I've asked this of
everybody that's coming in here.

00:14:58.597 --> 00:15:01.600 align:start position:0%
I mean, you mentioned how
it's, what did you say, it's an

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:03.702 align:start size:97% position:3%
old age home and a clown
circus, something like that,

00:15:03.702 --> 00:15:05.204
or a freak show.

00:15:05.204 --> 00:15:09.008
It's like is there anyone to
talk to that you can work with

00:15:09.008 --> 00:15:10.442
on things?

00:15:10.442 --> 00:15:11.443
- Oh, sure.

00:15:11.443 --> 00:15:12.278
- You know, on the other side?

00:15:12.278 --> 00:15:13.812
- Yeah.

00:15:13.812 --> 00:15:17.549
I mean there are people in
both parties to whom it is

00:15:18.951 --> 00:15:22.621 align:start size:97% position:3%
worthless to speak because
they are vessels through which

00:15:22.621 --> 00:15:24.123
the special interests operate,

00:15:24.123 --> 00:15:26.792 align:start position:0%
and the only thing that's gonna
guide them is some corrupt

00:15:26.792 --> 00:15:29.028
influence. Sometimes
it's campaign money.

00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:31.463
Sometimes it's some family
member of their's that gets a

00:15:31.463 --> 00:15:34.500 align:start size:94% position:6%
no-show job that
enhances their lifestyle.

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:36.635
Sometimes it's what their
stock portfolio looks like.

00:15:36.635 --> 00:15:40.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
But there are also very
good people on both sides,

00:15:40.639 --> 00:15:44.643
I would say, that want to
move the country forward.

00:15:44.643 --> 00:15:47.079
Increasingly, I think there
are too many Democrats who

00:15:47.079 --> 00:15:50.115 align:start size:88% position:13%
actually explicitly root
against the country,

00:15:50.115 --> 00:15:53.452
and they believe that
is virtuous because
they think that if

00:15:53.452 --> 00:15:57.656 align:start position:0%
America is weakened as a nation
that somehow we will treat

00:15:57.656 --> 00:15:59.525
each other better
and be more just,

00:15:59.525 --> 00:16:02.928
and they're actually just as
dumb as the neocons who believe

00:16:02.928 --> 00:16:05.597
that you can go behind every
Middle Eastern sand dune and

00:16:05.597 --> 00:16:08.968 align:start size:88% position:13%
build some Jeffersonian
democracy after a
regime change war.

00:16:08.968 --> 00:16:11.737
I'm not for regime change there,

00:16:11.737 --> 00:16:16.008
and I'm not for changing the
fundamentals of American life

00:16:16.008 --> 00:16:18.277
that have made us the
envy of the world.

00:16:18.277 --> 00:16:21.580 align:start position:0%
I think that the deconstruction
is occurring on the left

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:24.583
right now to the family,
to the education setting,

00:16:24.583 --> 00:16:26.285
to law enforcement,

00:16:26.285 --> 00:16:28.887 align:start size:97% position:3%
and as conservatives we have
to unite to resist that.

00:16:28.887 --> 00:16:32.124
- Do you think there's anything
that the Democrats can do to

00:16:32.124 --> 00:16:34.193 align:start size:97% position:3%
reverse that, or do you
think that ship has sailed?

00:16:34.193 --> 00:16:36.395
Meaning, we played a
clip this morning...

00:16:36.395 --> 00:16:38.597
- They don't invite me
to their bunco parties.

00:16:38.597 --> 00:16:40.833
- You know, we played
a clip this morning,

00:16:40.833 --> 00:16:42.334
It's like AOC,

00:16:42.334 --> 00:16:44.403
you guys passed the parents'
rights and education thing and

00:16:44.403 --> 00:16:46.171
she's calling it fascism.

00:16:46.171 --> 00:16:48.040 align:start size:97% position:3%
Hakeem Jeffries is saying
they don't want to teach about

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:49.208
the Holocaust.

00:16:49.208 --> 00:16:50.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's just like
lies and craziness.

00:16:50.676 --> 00:16:53.812
Is there anyone in there
that can sort of stem that,

00:16:53.812 --> 00:16:55.314 align:start size:84% position:16%
or do you think
that's just about...

00:16:55.314 --> 00:16:57.216
- Well, broadly, I don't know
the answer to that question,

00:16:57.216 --> 00:17:02.154
but I do think we have to be
willing to create alliances

00:17:02.154 --> 00:17:06.091 align:start size:94% position:6%
around issue sets where
we may have common ground.

00:17:06.091 --> 00:17:09.228 align:start position:0%
Take, for example, the
surveillance state that we have.

00:17:09.228 --> 00:17:12.798 align:start size:97% position:3%
I get real squeamish about the
powers that the Patriot Act

00:17:12.798 --> 00:17:16.735 align:start size:97% position:3%
and other laws have fused into
the national security state

00:17:16.735 --> 00:17:19.972
to deprive Americans of their
liberties often in the absence

00:17:19.972 --> 00:17:21.607
of constitutional protections.

00:17:21.607 --> 00:17:26.211
And I can find Democrats like
Zoe Lofgren on the Judiciary

00:17:26.211 --> 00:17:29.648
Committee, or Ro Khanna in
the progressive delegation,

00:17:29.648 --> 00:17:32.584
who will actually work with
me to try to build enduring

00:17:32.584 --> 00:17:36.422
coalitions against
an establishment that
also includes both

00:17:36.422 --> 00:17:38.057
Republicans and Democrats.

00:17:38.057 --> 00:17:40.426
I don't know why we're in Syria.

00:17:40.426 --> 00:17:42.694
I think that that is a
foolish conquest for the

00:17:42.694 --> 00:17:44.163
United States.

00:17:44.163 --> 00:17:45.831 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that our service
members are sitting ducks in

00:17:45.831 --> 00:17:49.968
many cases guarding oil rigs
where for relatively low cost

00:17:49.968 --> 00:17:52.404
the Iranians can send
lethal drone strikes.

00:17:52.404 --> 00:17:56.208
So I force a vote on whether
or not to withdraw from Syria.

00:17:56.208 --> 00:17:57.443
I get 20 minutes a time,

00:17:57.443 --> 00:17:58.710
the Republicans get
20 minutes a time,

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:00.312
the Democrats get
20 minutes a time.

00:18:00.312 --> 00:18:03.282
All of the time controlled
by each of the parties was

00:18:03.282 --> 00:18:06.118
given to people to debate in
favor of America staying in

00:18:06.118 --> 00:18:06.952
Syria.

00:18:06.952 --> 00:18:08.420
- Right.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:10.689 align:start position:0%
- It shows you on some of these
issues from surveillance to

00:18:10.689 --> 00:18:13.792 align:start position:0%
foreign policy how it's not
really the red team against the

00:18:13.792 --> 00:18:15.394
blue team.

00:18:15.394 --> 00:18:18.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's more about the
establishment against
the rest of us.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:19.865 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You must be kind of
proud that it does seem,

00:18:19.865 --> 00:18:23.335 align:start size:97% position:3%
at least from what I can tell,
that the anti-war sentiment

00:18:23.335 --> 00:18:25.871
does seem to be picking up
that, you know, even if you

00:18:25.871 --> 00:18:29.741 align:start position:0%
watched the way when Tucker
took all the statements from the

00:18:29.741 --> 00:18:32.478
presidential candidates
about Ukraine, you know,

00:18:32.478 --> 00:18:35.180
and DeSantis was a little more
in the Trump camp than people

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:36.715 align:start size:84% position:16%
wanted, then he
slightly moved on it.

00:18:36.715 --> 00:18:38.784
But the fact that the whole
machine came down on him...

00:18:38.784 --> 00:18:40.018
- He was right.

00:18:40.018 --> 00:18:42.187
- I mean, you're like
a DeSantis apostle.

00:18:42.187 --> 00:18:45.324
Why is it that Ron moved
away from the America first

00:18:45.324 --> 00:18:47.226 align:start position:0%
position and had to start
sounding more like Piers Morgan?

00:18:47.226 --> 00:18:49.228 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, it's interesting and
people can watch my video

00:18:49.228 --> 00:18:50.462
from the next day.

00:18:50.462 --> 00:18:53.165
I thought the statement
on Tucker was perfect.

00:18:53.165 --> 00:18:54.900
I actually thought it
was basically perfect.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:56.368
It's a territorial dispute.

00:18:56.368 --> 00:18:57.603
We can't solve all
the world's problems,

00:18:57.603 --> 00:18:58.537
and we've got our own problems.

00:18:58.537 --> 00:19:00.706
That seemed good enough to me.

00:19:00.706 --> 00:19:02.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
I should say I'm not a
surrogate for him or anything,

00:19:02.541 --> 00:19:04.643
so it is what it is.

00:19:04.643 --> 00:19:05.511
He moved off it a little bit.

00:19:05.511 --> 00:19:07.346
What did he say after?

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:11.049 align:start size:97% position:3%
Yeah, I'm not even sure, but
I know just over the last

00:19:11.049 --> 00:19:11.950
couple of days...

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:13.152
Oh, the Piers interview.

00:19:13.152 --> 00:19:14.620
He said "war criminal,"

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:16.021
and I think that's
what got everybody.

00:19:16.021 --> 00:19:17.689
He said Putin's a war criminal.

00:19:17.689 --> 00:19:21.493 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And he started to parrot
some McCain/Romney lines also.

00:19:21.493 --> 00:19:22.394
- Oh, the gas station line.

00:19:22.394 --> 00:19:23.262
That was the big one.

00:19:23.262 --> 00:19:24.530
Yeah.

00:19:24.530 --> 00:19:25.464
I don't know. I don't know.

00:19:25.464 --> 00:19:26.999
What do you think?

00:19:26.999 --> 00:19:30.102 align:start position:0%
- I think that DeSantis probably
had the right energy in the

00:19:30.102 --> 00:19:33.071
Tucker interview more than in
the Piers Morgan interview.

00:19:33.071 --> 00:19:37.476 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he and I served
together in the Congress,

00:19:37.476 --> 00:19:40.946 align:start position:0%
and I enthusiastically supported
his campaign for governor,

00:19:40.946 --> 00:19:44.583 align:start size:97% position:3%
served as his transition chair
when he took the position.

00:19:44.583 --> 00:19:46.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
Very proud of the work
that he's done in Florida.

00:19:46.919 --> 00:19:49.354 align:start size:97% position:3%
But we always would squabble
a bit over foreign policy

00:19:49.354 --> 00:19:52.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
because I read his book
and it leaned a little more

00:19:52.658 --> 00:19:56.929
into the Boltonista view of
the world than my view of the

00:19:56.929 --> 00:20:00.265 align:start position:0%
world, and so we would have
friendly squabbles about foreign

00:20:00.265 --> 00:20:01.333
policy at times.

00:20:01.333 --> 00:20:02.568
He's very bright.

00:20:02.568 --> 00:20:04.036
He understands the
world very well.

00:20:04.036 --> 00:20:08.373 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I have a different view
of what America's focus

00:20:08.373 --> 00:20:10.008
should be.

00:20:10.008 --> 00:20:12.711 align:start size:97% position:3%
And when we try to become
the world's policeman and the

00:20:12.711 --> 00:20:16.582 align:start size:97% position:3%
world's piggy bank, not only
does that drain us of our

00:20:16.582 --> 00:20:19.117
treasure and the blood
of our bravest patriots,

00:20:19.117 --> 00:20:20.419
it's often counterproductive.

00:20:20.419 --> 00:20:21.653
- Yeah. I'll be totally honest.

00:20:21.653 --> 00:20:23.121 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, and this is
what I said to Crenshaw,

00:20:23.121 --> 00:20:27.359 align:start position:0%
who I happen to agree more
with you on this than Crenshaw,

00:20:27.359 --> 00:20:30.362 align:start position:0%
to me, if nothing else, putting
aside all of the policies,

00:20:30.362 --> 00:20:33.999
we are governed by a bunch of
complete morons at the moment

00:20:33.999 --> 00:20:35.400
basically, so why
would I trust...

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:37.302 align:start size:84% position:16%
They can't do
anything right here.

00:20:37.302 --> 00:20:39.304 align:start size:97% position:3%
Why would I trust that we
can just endlessly give money

00:20:39.304 --> 00:20:41.173 align:start size:94% position:6%
across the world and
that's gonna fix anything,

00:20:41.173 --> 00:20:44.776 align:start position:0%
even if you put aside the
philosophic isolationist versus

00:20:44.776 --> 00:20:45.611
doing stuff policy.

00:20:45.611 --> 00:20:47.079
Yeah.

00:20:47.079 --> 00:20:51.049 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I also get to have spirited
intelligent debates with

00:20:51.049 --> 00:20:53.218 align:start size:97% position:3%
Mr. Crenshaw on the subject
and I enjoy those and I

00:20:53.218 --> 00:20:55.020
appreciate his perspective,

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:57.589
but you look at
our military now.

00:20:57.589 --> 00:20:59.057 align:start size:84% position:16%
During the Afghanistan
withdrawal,

00:20:59.057 --> 00:21:01.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
the fact that they
sequenced it incorrectly,

00:21:01.193 --> 00:21:03.595 align:start position:0%
the fact that they didn't
downstream the authority to the

00:21:03.595 --> 00:21:07.332
American snipers to take out
the suicide bomber resulted in

00:21:07.332 --> 00:21:09.067
the loss of 13 lives.

00:21:09.067 --> 00:21:12.137
Then we didn't even get
the redemption right.

00:21:12.137 --> 00:21:14.406
When we were gonna go have the
vengeance killing against the

00:21:14.406 --> 00:21:17.976
ISIS guys who were planning
the plot against Americans,

00:21:17.976 --> 00:21:21.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
we accidentally killed
a family in a van.

00:21:21.413 --> 00:21:25.584 align:start size:97% position:3%
So if Taiwan is invaded by
China and we get in the fight,

00:21:25.584 --> 00:21:27.252 align:start size:84% position:16%
are we're gonna
miss and hit Japan.

00:21:27.252 --> 00:21:28.720
- Right.

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:30.489 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I look at our military right
now and I'm inspired every

00:21:30.489 --> 00:21:33.959
day by the folks who put the
uniform on and love our nation.

00:21:33.959 --> 00:21:35.460
- Yes.

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:38.897
- But they are not well served
by the clowns who are making

00:21:38.897 --> 00:21:42.034
the calls at the senior
levels of the Pentagon.

00:21:42.034 --> 00:21:43.568
What did these guys get right?

00:21:43.568 --> 00:21:46.571
They told us that the Ghani
government was gonna stay and

00:21:46.571 --> 00:21:48.206
fight for at least six months.

00:21:48.206 --> 00:21:50.676 align:start position:0%
And they told us that the
Ukrainians were gonna be gone in

00:21:50.676 --> 00:21:52.177
six weeks.

00:21:52.177 --> 00:21:53.879
Do you trust these people to
make accurate assessments about

00:21:53.879 --> 00:21:55.314 align:start size:81% position:19%
literally anything
at this point?

00:21:55.314 --> 00:21:56.848
- No, I don't.

00:21:56.848 --> 00:21:58.483
So the other thing I've been
asking everybody is do you

00:21:58.483 --> 00:22:02.721
think this is incompetence
or willful destruction of

00:22:04.089 --> 00:22:06.892
everything that makes
sense. How about that?

00:22:06.892 --> 00:22:08.894 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I struggle with that
decision because, honestly,

00:22:08.894 --> 00:22:11.463
I try to not judge
people's motives.

00:22:11.463 --> 00:22:12.964
- Yeah.

00:22:12.964 --> 00:22:15.000
- But at some point, the good
book says you have to judge

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.368
the tree by the fruit.

00:22:16.368 --> 00:22:19.137
And I'm looking at
the decision calculus

00:22:19.137 --> 00:22:20.639
and what it leads to,

00:22:20.639 --> 00:22:24.843
and it is just unworthy of
my constituents who will go

00:22:24.843 --> 00:22:28.447 align:start position:0%
anywhere and fight anyone
because they love this country,

00:22:28.447 --> 00:22:30.449
and they don't ask
the tough questions.

00:22:30.449 --> 00:22:32.317
They do whatever they're told.

00:22:32.317 --> 00:22:35.620
And so it is incumbent upon
us to be willing to ask those

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:37.122
questions.

00:22:37.122 --> 00:22:40.359
And when Secretary of Defense
and the chairman come before

00:22:40.359 --> 00:22:43.095 align:start size:84% position:16%
the House Armed
Services Committee, I
think I'll be asking

00:22:43.095 --> 00:22:44.563
some tough questions.

00:22:44.563 --> 00:22:46.398
- So I think one thing
that people see is, okay,

00:22:46.398 --> 00:22:49.768 align:start size:97% position:3%
so you bring out a bunch of
people to talk about big tech,

00:22:49.768 --> 00:22:52.070
the Twitter executives,
or this one or that one,

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:54.373
and we expose some
of the nonsense,

00:22:54.373 --> 00:22:56.708 align:start size:84% position:16%
but nothing ever
happens after, right?

00:22:56.708 --> 00:22:58.243
That it's just like, okay, wow,

00:22:58.243 --> 00:23:01.747 align:start size:97% position:3%
you got the guy who traded the
email with the FBI agent to

00:23:01.747 --> 00:23:04.483
censor somebody, but then the
next day it's like everyone

00:23:04.483 --> 00:23:05.684
still has their jobs.

00:23:05.684 --> 00:23:08.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
No one in government
was hauled out.

00:23:08.854 --> 00:23:10.389
So what about that?

00:23:10.389 --> 00:23:12.257
What about getting some of
the people that were doing

00:23:12.257 --> 00:23:14.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
some of the surveillance
stuff that was involved with

00:23:14.559 --> 00:23:16.428
Twitter and Facebook and
everything else and getting

00:23:16.428 --> 00:23:17.929
them to testify.

00:23:17.929 --> 00:23:19.398
Aren't those the people
that should be testifying

00:23:19.398 --> 00:23:22.200
not just the kind of half-assed
Twitter executive who was or

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:24.503
wasn't doing the best he could?

00:23:24.503 --> 00:23:26.104
- Well, I think that's
what we're building to.

00:23:26.104 --> 00:23:27.606
- Yeah.

00:23:27.606 --> 00:23:31.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
- My assessment is that
that is precisely how this

00:23:31.042 --> 00:23:32.711
investigation crescendos.

00:23:32.711 --> 00:23:35.547
The people in our government
who believe that they should

00:23:35.547 --> 00:23:38.917
substitute their judgment about
what is true or false, about

00:23:38.917 --> 00:23:43.121
what is disinformation or not,
for the judgment of Americans

00:23:43.121 --> 00:23:46.825
who I think actually can
compare sources and review

00:23:46.825 --> 00:23:47.759
information.

00:23:47.759 --> 00:23:49.561
Like the pro-censorship crowd.

00:23:49.561 --> 00:23:51.663
They have such a low
view of the rest of us.

00:23:51.663 --> 00:23:52.898
- Yeah.

00:23:52.898 --> 00:23:54.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
- They have to be
there to protect us.

00:23:54.833 --> 00:23:57.202 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well actually that's
not how it works at all.

00:23:57.202 --> 00:24:00.038
And when you ask
about consequences,

00:24:00.038 --> 00:24:02.107
the United States Congress
cannot go throw handcuffs

00:24:02.107 --> 00:24:04.743 align:start size:97% position:3%
on anyone, and that's probably
for pretty good reason.

00:24:04.743 --> 00:24:07.512
Right now we have a Justice
Department that is pretty

00:24:07.512 --> 00:24:09.748 align:start size:84% position:16%
recalcitrant to any
referral that we make.

00:24:09.748 --> 00:24:12.484
Even if these people tell us
to get bent when we send them

00:24:12.484 --> 00:24:15.287
subpoenas, that'll be
an interesting fight.

00:24:15.287 --> 00:24:18.723
And so we have to go through
this accommodation process to

00:24:18.723 --> 00:24:22.527 align:start size:97% position:3%
try to bleed out of the
executive branch the people,

00:24:22.527 --> 00:24:25.564
the records, the admissions
that I think make the case.

00:24:25.564 --> 00:24:28.099 align:start size:88% position:13%
The real question is
how to use the leverage,

00:24:28.099 --> 00:24:30.735
which I believe is the
power of the subpoena

00:24:30.735 --> 00:24:32.270
and the power of the purse.

00:24:32.270 --> 00:24:35.841
That is what Speaker McCarthy
said in his acceptance speech,

00:24:35.841 --> 00:24:38.743 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it was beautiful
and it's true.

00:24:38.743 --> 00:24:42.781
And so we cannot continue to
fund this weaponized government

00:24:42.781 --> 00:24:46.151
against our people if those
authorities stay in place.

00:24:46.151 --> 00:24:49.554
And if we do not bake into any
budget deal a restriction on

00:24:49.554 --> 00:24:51.523
what they're able to do to us.

00:24:51.523 --> 00:24:53.758
- What about the fact that it
seems like so few people that

00:24:53.758 --> 00:24:55.861 align:start position:0%
are asking the questions at
these hearings have any freaking

00:24:55.861 --> 00:24:57.295
clue what they're talking about?

00:24:57.295 --> 00:24:59.598
It's almost not everything,

00:24:59.598 --> 00:25:00.799 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I would say
what it comes to...

00:25:00.799 --> 00:25:02.000 align:start size:84% position:16%
- They should just
yield me all the time.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:03.335 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's what you're
saying, right?

00:25:03.335 --> 00:25:04.569
That's allowed.

00:25:04.569 --> 00:25:06.037
They could just
send me the time.

00:25:06.037 --> 00:25:06.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Let Gaetz do it.
He's a little younger.

00:25:06.872 --> 00:25:07.806
- Jordan.

00:25:07.806 --> 00:25:08.974
There's a bunch of good ones.

00:25:08.974 --> 00:25:11.743 align:start size:88% position:13%
Mike Johnson, Dan
Bishop, Harriet Hageman.

00:25:11.743 --> 00:25:12.744
We've got a lot of good ones.

00:25:12.744 --> 00:25:15.046
- Yeah. She's something else.

00:25:15.046 --> 00:25:16.114
- She's very effective.

00:25:16.114 --> 00:25:17.349
- Yeah.

00:25:17.349 --> 00:25:18.850
But really, especially
with the text stuff,

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:21.186
you get these people up there
that clearly don't even know

00:25:21.186 --> 00:25:23.688 align:start size:97% position:3%
how to open up an app on
their phone and they're like,

00:25:23.688 --> 00:25:25.490
it's just so stupid.

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:29.327 align:start size:97% position:3%
- We are not going to be able
to defeat big tech if our

00:25:29.327 --> 00:25:32.831
leaders on the front line
are not digital natives.

00:25:32.831 --> 00:25:35.634 align:start position:0%
That's why this next generation
coming behind me is so

00:25:35.634 --> 00:25:37.836 align:start position:0%
important to win the battle
against big tech and to win the

00:25:37.836 --> 00:25:41.473 align:start size:97% position:3%
battles against transhumanism
that are not even fully

00:25:41.473 --> 00:25:43.275
emerging yet, right?

00:25:43.275 --> 00:25:48.280 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I view the geriatric
millennials among us as really

00:25:49.748 --> 00:25:51.783
the bridge to try to start the
fires that the Zoomers behind

00:25:51.783 --> 00:25:54.953
us will have to really take
up because it fundamentally

00:25:54.953 --> 00:25:56.221
shapes their life.

00:25:56.221 --> 00:25:57.789
But if you've been
waiting around...

00:25:57.789 --> 00:25:59.891 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Geriatric millennial,
that's a Gen-Z'er basically?

00:25:59.891 --> 00:26:01.960 align:start size:88% position:13%
- No, no. Geriatric
millennials are like the

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:04.329 align:start size:97% position:3%
old millennials, like the
first millennials, you know.

00:26:04.329 --> 00:26:05.564
- Right.

00:26:05.564 --> 00:26:07.065 align:start size:81% position:19%
So where does that
put Gen-Z then?

00:26:07.065 --> 00:26:08.900
- Well, Gen-Z, they're the
Zoomers, they're behind us.

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:10.702
- Oh, I'm sorry. Not
Gen-Z, I mean Gen-X.

00:26:10.702 --> 00:26:11.536
I'm Gen-X.

00:26:11.536 --> 00:26:12.370
What happened to us?

00:26:12.370 --> 00:26:13.638
- Yeah, no. Gen-X.

00:26:13.638 --> 00:26:15.907
You guys listened to
all the metal bands,

00:26:15.907 --> 00:26:18.476 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you're not really digital
natives at all, right?

00:26:18.476 --> 00:26:22.113
I mean, Gen-X actually believes
that you should come to work

00:26:22.113 --> 00:26:24.683
early and stay late and that
the way you moved up in the

00:26:24.683 --> 00:26:26.618
world was by working harder.

00:26:26.618 --> 00:26:30.288
You guys were getting into the
workforce in the Reagan '80s.

00:26:30.288 --> 00:26:33.058
The Gen-Xers have no idea
how to talk to the Zoomers,

00:26:33.058 --> 00:26:35.360
who show up and say like, "I'd
like to discuss my work-life

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:36.761
balance with you."

00:26:36.761 --> 00:26:39.931 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's totally foreign
to the Gen-Xers.

00:26:39.931 --> 00:26:43.768
But for us, here's the
key policy question.

00:26:43.768 --> 00:26:47.973
Do you carve up big tech in
the courts or do you reshape

00:26:47.973 --> 00:26:49.574
these companies legislatively?

00:26:49.574 --> 00:26:52.611
And frankly it's what divides
people like me and Jim Jordan.

00:26:52.611 --> 00:26:54.112
- Right.

00:26:54.112 --> 00:26:56.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
- The Jim Jordan approach
would be strip them of their

00:26:56.448 --> 00:26:59.918
immunities like section 230 and
then allow the trial lawyers

00:26:59.918 --> 00:27:01.419
to go feast on them.

00:27:01.419 --> 00:27:04.789
It's kind of like what we did
to big tobacco in the '90s,

00:27:04.789 --> 00:27:08.526
and I just don't know if that
is enough firepower to shoot

00:27:08.526 --> 00:27:10.595
at the Death Star
that is big tech.

00:27:10.595 --> 00:27:14.032 align:start size:97% position:3%
I believe we have to go more
the direction of the Bell

00:27:14.032 --> 00:27:17.902 align:start size:88% position:13%
breakup in telecom where
you literally force,

00:27:17.902 --> 00:27:20.205
but you use force of
law, to say that Meta

00:27:20.205 --> 00:27:22.273
has to alienate Instagram.

00:27:22.273 --> 00:27:24.175
You say that Google has
to alienate YouTube,

00:27:24.175 --> 00:27:27.445
and you have to reshape and
break up these companies

00:27:27.445 --> 00:27:31.116 align:start size:97% position:3%
without going through the
litigation process for another

00:27:31.116 --> 00:27:32.651
seven, eight years.

00:27:32.651 --> 00:27:34.452
- Is this where you would say
that old school republicanism

00:27:34.452 --> 00:27:35.620
just kind of doesn't work?

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:37.088
- Yes.

00:27:37.088 --> 00:27:38.657
- We played a clip on my show
about two, three weeks ago

00:27:38.657 --> 00:27:41.026
where Mike Pence
was on CNBC saying,

00:27:41.026 --> 00:27:43.928
Well, DeSantis went too far
by taking away some special

00:27:43.928 --> 00:27:45.363
rights from Disney.

00:27:45.363 --> 00:27:46.598
And it was like,
well wait a minute.

00:27:46.598 --> 00:27:47.465
- The government gave
Disney those rights.

00:27:47.465 --> 00:27:49.000
- Exactly, exactly.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:52.804
And it just read to me as
so out of touch with what

00:27:52.804 --> 00:27:54.406
people want, right?

00:27:55.540 --> 00:27:56.474
There's also Sea World.

00:27:56.474 --> 00:27:57.842
They don't have special rights.

00:27:57.842 --> 00:27:58.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
- What is the constituency
for Mike Pence?

00:27:58.410 --> 00:27:59.244
- Right.

00:27:59.244 --> 00:28:00.111
- I'm just wondering.

00:28:00.111 --> 00:28:01.579
- I don't know.

00:28:01.579 --> 00:28:04.449 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Other than Sunday show
bookers and like CNN and the

00:28:04.449 --> 00:28:05.650
mainstream media...

00:28:05.650 --> 00:28:06.584
- I think he knows that.

00:28:06.584 --> 00:28:07.452
I don't think he's running.

00:28:07.452 --> 00:28:08.286
- You don't?

00:28:08.286 --> 00:28:09.120
- I don't think so.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:10.055
I think he knows that.

00:28:10.055 --> 00:28:12.057
I don't know anyone.

00:28:12.057 --> 00:28:13.091
What is that?

00:28:13.091 --> 00:28:14.292
- Okay.

00:28:14.292 --> 00:28:15.827 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm wondering where I've
gotta go to meet the

00:28:15.827 --> 00:28:17.829
Mike Pence voter.

00:28:17.829 --> 00:28:19.698
- Maybe Mike Pence's house.

00:28:19.698 --> 00:28:20.932
- You must.

00:28:20.932 --> 00:28:21.866
- All right.

00:28:21.866 --> 00:28:23.101
So you're a Florida congressman.

00:28:23.101 --> 00:28:26.137
You're torn with the
freaking red-assed...

00:28:26.137 --> 00:28:28.473
Where did you get the jacket
like that? That red jacket?

00:28:28.473 --> 00:28:32.043 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Oh, I lifted it off
of a maitre'd on the way

00:28:32.043 --> 00:28:33.878
into the event.

00:28:33.878 --> 00:28:35.513
- I actually believe you.

00:28:35.513 --> 00:28:38.049 align:start size:84% position:16%
I sort of do believe
you, but okay.

00:28:38.049 --> 00:28:39.651 align:start size:84% position:16%
Look, you're a
Florida congressman,

00:28:39.651 --> 00:28:41.953
you obviously like DeSantis
but you're doing your thing

00:28:41.953 --> 00:28:43.354
with Trump.

00:28:43.354 --> 00:28:45.356 align:start size:97% position:3%
From where I sit as
someone that lived in Cali,

00:28:45.356 --> 00:28:46.291
moved to Florida,

00:28:46.291 --> 00:28:47.792
my life is immeasurably better.

00:28:47.792 --> 00:28:49.861
I brought two companies here
that are both flourishing.

00:28:49.861 --> 00:28:52.163
Every one of my guys
over here all moved.

00:28:52.163 --> 00:28:53.998 align:start size:97% position:3%
Everyone is seeing the
fruits of all of that stuff.

00:28:53.998 --> 00:28:55.533 align:start size:97% position:3%
- By that standard, you
could vote for any Republican

00:28:55.533 --> 00:28:57.202
governor in America.

00:28:58.636 --> 00:29:00.472
- Well, I could certainly go
to any Republican state and be

00:29:00.472 --> 00:29:01.506
better than it was in Cali.

00:29:01.506 --> 00:29:02.340
- Sure.

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:03.708
- For sure.

00:29:03.708 --> 00:29:05.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm just seeing everything
work in Florida.

00:29:05.877 --> 00:29:09.013
So how do you balance that in
that you live in a state that

00:29:09.013 --> 00:29:10.782
clearly the governor's
doing the right thing,

00:29:10.782 --> 00:29:13.485 align:start position:0%
you've got the super majorities
you want and it's cooking,

00:29:13.485 --> 00:29:15.887
and then also obviously that
you like Trump and he obviously

00:29:15.887 --> 00:29:18.523
has unfinished business
and that whole thing.

00:29:18.523 --> 00:29:20.925
- Well, I am a DeSantis admirer.

00:29:20.925 --> 00:29:23.194
I worked very hard
to get him elected,

00:29:23.194 --> 00:29:27.198 align:start position:0%
but Florida has been under
unified Republican control since

00:29:27.198 --> 00:29:28.733
the mid-90s.

00:29:28.733 --> 00:29:32.303 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so there has been a
system in place in Florida to

00:29:32.303 --> 00:29:35.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
advance school choice, to
have right sized environmental

00:29:35.006 --> 00:29:38.409
regulations, to not overspend
on the state budget, to care

00:29:38.409 --> 00:29:40.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
for our seniors
and the vulnerable.

00:29:40.278 --> 00:29:43.748 align:start size:97% position:3%
And Ron DeSantis has done
an exquisite job as governor

00:29:43.748 --> 00:29:45.350
leading that enterprise.

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:48.820
I know because I worked with
him as his transition chairman

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:51.623
and helped him hire some of
the great Floridians who've

00:29:51.623 --> 00:29:54.192
helped him enact bold policies.

00:29:55.593 --> 00:29:59.597 align:start size:97% position:3%
The time is right for Trump
in Washington right now

00:29:59.597 --> 00:30:01.332
because that is a
different fight.

00:30:01.332 --> 00:30:05.436 align:start position:0%
Being able to successfully
steer a successful state and to

00:30:05.436 --> 00:30:08.273 align:start position:0%
chart a bullish course is
something I'm glad Ron's doing.

00:30:08.273 --> 00:30:12.410 align:start size:97% position:3%
I hope he continues to do
it for his full eight years.

00:30:12.410 --> 00:30:16.714
This place needs somebody
to come in with a sense of

00:30:18.183 --> 00:30:22.720
retribution and a sense of, I
think, fight that is unique to

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:24.389
President Trump.

00:30:24.389 --> 00:30:27.292
And being in Waco
this last weekend,

00:30:27.292 --> 00:30:29.961 align:start size:97% position:3%
seeing the tens of
thousands of people show up,

00:30:29.961 --> 00:30:32.897 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's something unique about
Trump and politics that I

00:30:32.897 --> 00:30:36.134
think we need to have even a
chance against what we face

00:30:36.134 --> 00:30:37.669
electorally.

00:30:37.669 --> 00:30:39.337 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So I'll say to you what I've
been saying all along on my

00:30:39.337 --> 00:30:41.172 align:start size:84% position:16%
show, which is, again,
I like 'em both.

00:30:41.172 --> 00:30:43.474
If it ends up that Trump is
the president and DeSantis

00:30:43.474 --> 00:30:45.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
remains my governor, I'll
be freaking thrilled.

00:30:45.176 --> 00:30:47.345 align:start size:84% position:16%
That might be the
best situation truly,

00:30:47.345 --> 00:30:49.214 align:start size:97% position:3%
and as a Floridian I can
fully make an argument that he

00:30:49.214 --> 00:30:50.048
shouldn't run.

00:30:50.048 --> 00:30:51.749
I fully get all that.

00:30:53.184 --> 00:30:54.986
But what I would like to
see out of Trump right now,

00:30:54.986 --> 00:30:57.822 align:start size:97% position:3%
since you have his ear, is
that you don't have to destroy

00:30:57.822 --> 00:30:59.624
the guy like he's
trying to do it.

00:30:59.624 --> 00:31:02.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
It seems to me that the
nicknames are not working,

00:31:02.126 --> 00:31:03.461 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the pretending
that Florida...

00:31:03.461 --> 00:31:05.864 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You don't think
Meatball Ron worked?

00:31:05.864 --> 00:31:06.931
- Not really.

00:31:06.931 --> 00:31:07.866
- You didn't?

00:31:07.866 --> 00:31:09.167
John Oliver thought it worked.

00:31:09.167 --> 00:31:10.568
This is a hot take, Dave.

00:31:10.568 --> 00:31:12.237
- I don't agree with
John Oliver a lot.

00:31:12.237 --> 00:31:13.838
- I don't agree with
John Oliver a lot.

00:31:13.838 --> 00:31:15.840 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But you're agreeing
with John Oliver.

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:16.674
That's a problem.
That's a problem.

00:31:16.674 --> 00:31:18.176
- Listen.

00:31:18.176 --> 00:31:19.577
I'm asking the question if
you thought Meatball Ron

00:31:19.577 --> 00:31:20.812
just worked as a concept.

00:31:20.812 --> 00:31:22.614 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It might be the
best of all of them,

00:31:22.614 --> 00:31:24.249
but like desanctimonious.

00:31:24.249 --> 00:31:26.918
Come on. It doesn't work.
He's not sanctimonious.

00:31:26.918 --> 00:31:28.987 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm not sure Trump knows
what the word means.

00:31:28.987 --> 00:31:31.089
- Look, sometimes you gotta
go through a few iterations

00:31:31.089 --> 00:31:32.457
on a nickname in the game
before you land at one.

00:31:32.457 --> 00:31:33.391
- Okay.

00:31:33.391 --> 00:31:34.826
But putting aside the nickname,

00:31:34.826 --> 00:31:36.494 align:start size:97% position:3%
the point of trying to
destroy the guy that is the

00:31:36.494 --> 00:31:40.031 align:start size:97% position:3%
standard bearer of a
Republican as governor I think

00:31:40.031 --> 00:31:42.333
is just misguided.

00:31:42.333 --> 00:31:43.468
I think that that's misguided.

00:31:43.468 --> 00:31:44.936
I get politics is nasty.

00:31:44.936 --> 00:31:47.472 align:start position:0%
- Yeah. Nobody pitches underhand
here. It's the presidency.

00:31:47.472 --> 00:31:48.907
- Yeah.

00:31:48.907 --> 00:31:52.477 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I think that when someone
puts himself forward

00:31:52.477 --> 00:31:53.745
for the contest...

00:31:53.745 --> 00:31:54.579 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm telling you I'm
hearing a lot of that,

00:31:54.579 --> 00:31:55.346
that people are like...

00:31:55.346 --> 00:31:56.180
- Oh sure.

00:31:56.180 --> 00:31:57.749
None of us like that.

00:31:57.749 --> 00:32:00.018
None of us like when folks
we admire are disagreeing

00:32:00.018 --> 00:32:00.785
and fighting with one another.

00:32:00.785 --> 00:32:02.186
- Yeah.

00:32:02.186 --> 00:32:04.222 align:start position:0%
- But I certainly don't
begrudge either for engaging in

00:32:04.222 --> 00:32:05.924 align:start size:88% position:13%
that enterprise because
it's competitive.

00:32:05.924 --> 00:32:08.226
Look, I mean, you know how
you get the strongest lions,

00:32:08.226 --> 00:32:11.029 align:start size:97% position:3%
because if there's a strong
male lion and he comes up on a

00:32:11.029 --> 00:32:13.965
less strong male lion that's
got some female lions around,

00:32:13.965 --> 00:32:16.734 align:start size:97% position:3%
he kills the male lion and
mates with the females, right?

00:32:16.734 --> 00:32:18.303
That's how it goes in
the animal kingdom,

00:32:18.303 --> 00:32:20.305 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'm not entirely sure
that politics is all that much

00:32:20.305 --> 00:32:21.839
different.

00:32:21.839 --> 00:32:23.107
- Every now and again you end
up with a bunch of dead lions,

00:32:23.107 --> 00:32:24.976
and then you've got
Democrats in power,

00:32:24.976 --> 00:32:26.577
which is a bigger
problem probably.

00:32:26.577 --> 00:32:27.779
- Yeah.

00:32:27.779 --> 00:32:29.747 align:start size:88% position:13%
They're the hyenas I
think in in my metaphor.

00:32:29.747 --> 00:32:30.581
- Exactly.

00:32:32.684 --> 00:32:33.918
What's next?

00:32:33.918 --> 00:32:35.386
What's next for you?

00:32:35.386 --> 00:32:36.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, you're gonna fight
as this thing's going.

00:32:36.888 --> 00:32:38.856
It seems like Republicans are
kind of moving a little bit.

00:32:38.856 --> 00:32:40.625 align:start size:84% position:16%
You've got some stuff
out of McCarthy.

00:32:40.625 --> 00:32:42.627
What else do wanna
see in this town?

00:32:42.627 --> 00:32:44.662
- I'm thinking very short term.

00:32:44.662 --> 00:32:47.699
I'm thinking let's get a good
deal on the debt limit that

00:32:47.699 --> 00:32:50.368
does not bankrupt this country.

00:32:50.368 --> 00:32:53.838
I'm looking after that and an
appropriations process that I

00:32:53.838 --> 00:32:58.609
hope will reset the table for
a decade long past my time

00:32:58.609 --> 00:33:00.144
here as a congressman.

00:33:00.144 --> 00:33:03.348
And I hope that the things
that I fought for during the

00:33:03.348 --> 00:33:07.251
speaker's race will become the
expectation and the baseline

00:33:07.251 --> 00:33:08.786
going forward.

00:33:08.786 --> 00:33:11.389
And that the next generation
can come in with even better

00:33:11.389 --> 00:33:15.393
ideas about how to empower the
membership and how to really

00:33:15.393 --> 00:33:19.664
expose the extent to which we
don't get to make decisions on

00:33:19.664 --> 00:33:22.233
far too many matters that are
of great consequence to our

00:33:22.233 --> 00:33:23.434
constituents.

00:33:23.434 --> 00:33:25.603
And we've gotta do a
better job fixing that.

00:33:25.603 --> 00:33:27.538
And I want to use the
tools in the toolkit.

00:33:27.538 --> 00:33:29.040
I'm tired of staring at 'em.

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:33.044 align:start size:97% position:3%
I often get chastised by my
colleagues that I'm impatient,

00:33:33.044 --> 00:33:36.948 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I feel the anxiousness
of that in this moment.

00:33:36.948 --> 00:33:38.383
- That probably should be the
ending, but I feel like I've

00:33:38.383 --> 00:33:40.852 align:start size:97% position:3%
gotta ask you one more because
now having sat with you,

00:33:40.852 --> 00:33:43.554
it's, again, the first time
that we ever met or anything.

00:33:43.554 --> 00:33:45.256
You know if you listen to the
mainstream media talk about

00:33:45.256 --> 00:33:48.626
you, you're some
kind of far right.

00:33:48.626 --> 00:33:49.827
It's all nonsense, of course.

00:33:49.827 --> 00:33:51.029
They say that about
all of us, so...

00:33:51.029 --> 00:33:53.698
- Why do think they
hate me so much?

00:33:53.698 --> 00:33:54.866
I've wondered this.

00:33:54.866 --> 00:33:56.134
I don't fret over it.

00:33:56.134 --> 00:33:57.502
- Now it's probably
just like enough,

00:33:57.502 --> 00:33:58.536
you're around Trump enough,

00:33:58.536 --> 00:34:00.271
it doesn't matter what you do.

00:34:00.271 --> 00:34:01.506
Yeah. It just doesn't
matter what you say.

00:34:01.506 --> 00:34:03.007 align:start size:81% position:19%
But they think I'm
far right, too.

00:34:03.007 --> 00:34:04.675
- Oh yeah, you get it, too.

00:34:04.675 --> 00:34:06.577
- I mean, New York
Times literally.

00:34:06.577 --> 00:34:08.046
The front page of the
Sunday New York Times

00:34:08.046 --> 00:34:09.447 align:start size:94% position:6%
said I was one of the
leaders of the alt-right.

00:34:09.447 --> 00:34:11.315 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I could be stinking
you up, right?

00:34:11.315 --> 00:34:13.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Better than being one
of the followers, I guess.

00:34:13.317 --> 00:34:14.786
- Yeah. I guess. Exactly.

00:34:14.786 --> 00:34:15.887
At least then you have
some ability to move them

00:34:15.887 --> 00:34:17.789
or something.

00:34:17.789 --> 00:34:21.125
But what about that portion of
it that you're also fighting?

00:34:21.125 --> 00:34:23.628
It's not just that
you're fighting sort
of a deep state and

00:34:23.628 --> 00:34:26.464 align:start position:0%
a system that's screwy and
Democrats that have kind of lost

00:34:26.464 --> 00:34:30.968 align:start position:0%
their minds, but also just like
a media that is just so far

00:34:30.968 --> 00:34:34.405
from the truth that it makes
just the messaging part of it

00:34:34.405 --> 00:34:36.340
incredibly difficult.

00:34:36.340 --> 00:34:40.445
- When members get to this
town and they go through the

00:34:40.445 --> 00:34:44.148 align:start size:88% position:13%
first barrage of
unfair media criticism,

00:34:44.148 --> 00:34:47.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
I try to advise them don't
let the process be the

00:34:47.151 --> 00:34:48.619
punishment.

00:34:48.619 --> 00:34:51.889
And often that's why the
media does what they do.

00:34:51.889 --> 00:34:55.560 align:start size:84% position:16%
They want to shape
our legislative goals.

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:58.429 align:start position:0%
They wanna shape the things
that we're allowed to talk about

00:34:58.429 --> 00:34:59.864
in civil society.

00:34:59.864 --> 00:35:02.366
And I just don't give
in to the construct.

00:35:02.366 --> 00:35:05.937 align:start position:0%
And I actually have a far higher
view of my fellow Americans

00:35:05.937 --> 00:35:08.739
than I think the media does
because I think people see

00:35:08.739 --> 00:35:10.174
through the bullshit.

00:35:10.174 --> 00:35:12.677 align:start position:0%
I think people know who's
fighting for them and who isn't,

00:35:12.677 --> 00:35:16.314
and in a town where a lot of
the politicians are just actors

00:35:16.314 --> 00:35:19.250 align:start position:0%
reading the scripts from
lobbyists and staff who want to

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:23.821 align:start position:0%
become lobbyists, I think people
will forgive you a mistake

00:35:23.821 --> 00:35:27.158
and an occasional disagreement
if they feel you are sincere

00:35:27.158 --> 00:35:29.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that you care about them
and that you're actually

00:35:29.026 --> 00:35:31.229 align:start size:97% position:3%
fighting for them and that's
how you spend your time.

00:35:31.229 --> 00:35:32.964 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I try to convey
that in the work I do.

00:35:32.964 --> 00:35:34.866 align:start size:88% position:13%
I know the media
conveys something else,

00:35:34.866 --> 00:35:37.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I continue to get
overwhelmingly reelected.

00:35:37.535 --> 00:35:41.939
And I really am heartened
by the prayers that I get,

00:35:41.939 --> 00:35:45.009 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I feel from all over this
country from folks who want

00:35:45.009 --> 00:35:46.878
to see that our work is done,

00:35:46.878 --> 00:35:49.714
not for my sake, but for theirs.

00:35:49.714 --> 00:35:53.317 align:start position:0%
- I know we may not see totally
eye to eye on everything,

00:35:53.317 --> 00:35:55.786 align:start size:97% position:3%
but from one Floridian to
another, it's been a pleasure.

00:35:55.786 --> 00:35:56.654
- Oh, thanks for having me, man.

00:35:56.654 --> 00:35:58.156
- Yeah.

00:35:58.156 --> 00:35:59.690 align:start size:94% position:6%
- If you're looking for
more honest and thoughtful

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:02.126
conversations about politics
instead of mindless drivel,

